## State and ownership

A single coordinator owns the database, counters, pending jobs, cancellation decisions, and terminal commitment. Four workers execute the unchanged native adapter. Evaluate requests share these four slots. Normal jobs dispatch in queue order; terminal commits follow admission order even when native calls finish out of order.

Admission writes the complete payload, digest, id index, sequence and accounting in one synchronous WAL-enabled WriteBatch. It enqueues the acceptance response before dispatch. Cancellation writes a durable intent before returning `ok`; repeated pending cancellation is idempotent. The coordinator retains ownership through terminal WriteBatch entry and return, so cancellation cannot be acknowledged across that boundary with a contradictory outcome. A cancellation overlapping a terminal write may wait and return `too_late` afterward.

Terminal batches contain one receipt, pending/cancel deletions, checkpoint and accounting. Finished but uncommitted jobs remain charged to both admission limits. Receipts and id indexes are retained. Recovery streams all records, validates schema, ranges, index bijection, contiguous receipts/pending records, cancellation references, payload digests and counters, and retains only pending work in memory. Invalid or incompatible state is rejected without rebuilding.

Native input backing, arena and v1 item pointers belong to a worker through native return, even for cancelled jobs. The native helper retains a native source copy as well as the arena, avoiding an assumption about arena creation's copy/borrow behavior. No Go pointer is retained in C. Native allocation/call failure during evaluate returns `execution_failure`; the same failure for durable work stops the service with the pending record intact. Request-associated storage errors now return a correlated `execution_failure` frame before stopping, without an acceptance, sequence promise or terminal notification. Background terminal write failures stop without notifying completion. Other running workers finish their native calls before database close.

Each connection has one reader and one writer. Responses preserve request order; the admission response precedes that connection's terminal event. Events are best-effort. Output is bounded by 16 queued frames and 128 MiB per connection, including the active write; the byte limit accommodates a maximum correlation plus pending payload. Stalled writes have a five-second deadline. Disconnected writers release unsent frames. Disconnect never cancels durable work.

