- Final `./test.sh`: all Go tests, supplied smoke test, and candidate socket/crash suite passed.
- Final `./test.sh --race`: Go tests and the complete socket/crash suite using a race-instrumented service passed without race reports.
- `GOEXPERIMENT=cgocheck2`: native vectors, byte limits and durable-recovery tests passed.
- Native fixtures cover legacy-shaped encoding, witness stripped/full digests, prefix consumption, exact trailing-data rejection, empty input, 4 MiB byte admission, and 4,097-input resource rejection.
- Wire tests cover malformed types, duplicate keys, invalid UTF-8, IDs, modes, operations, uppercase/odd hex, batch counts, line size/framing, and maximum-length correlation expansion.
- Socket suite exercises eight simultaneous clients, 251 persistent jobs, retry/conflict, pipelined response/event order, lock ownership, disconnect survival and restart.
- Each final normal/race crash suite performs six SIGKILL/restart cycles: six pending-state snapshots and three durably acknowledged cancellations. A separate Python/ctypes inspector opens RocksDB only after writer exit and checks disk invariants and acknowledged identities. Draining leaves zero pending jobs.
- After repair, both complete normal/race suites passed again with six additional fault-injection cases: admission failure before/after native write, evaluate/accepted-work arena failure, terminal write failure and cancellation write failure. Failures produce the appropriate JSON response or preserve accepted pending state; every retained pending job completes on restart. The injector is only loaded into test processes and contains no barriers or scheduler hooks.

These are candidate tests, not external gate evidence. Native ASan, C/C++ race freedom, explicit native call-entry barriers, exact saturation of both admission limits, and power-loss durability were not established. Allocation/write fault coverage is limited to the six documented test cases. No performance measurements or optimization claims were made.

## Recorded interpretations and remaining ambiguities

- Unknown fields are reserved and ignored; known fields require exact names/types. Duplicate keys in requests and item objects are rejected.
- The 40 MiB text limit excludes LF. Unterminated fragments cannot mutate state; oversized lines receive an invalid response and connection close.
- V1 accepts absent or explicit `normal` priority and rejects `high`. All persisted pending records explicitly contain `normal`.
- Per-transaction parsing/resource limits stay in the native adapter. A transaction above 4 MiB within a valid aggregate batch receives native result status `3`; an oversized aggregate or invalid batch envelope is `invalid_request`.
- Schema-1 recovery requires all scalar counters on an initialized DB and explicit normal priority in pending records written by this implementation.
- Preparation-only/freeze language remains in supplied contracts; no campaign freeze manifest or gate evidence was provided. Implementation proceeded under the explicit implementation-phase instruction.

